 ## Overview
-This method partitions the 3D scene volume into non-overlapping tiles to enable distributed NeRF training across multiple GPUs by balancing spatial content density [S1]. Unlike Block-NeRF, which partitions based on training camera locations, this approach partitions the space directly using a Probability Density Function (PDF) derived from scene geometry, ensuring that regions with higher content density receive finer bounding boxes (a level-of-detail property) [S1]. It achieves this by recursively splitting the volume along axes that minimize the aspect ratio of the resulting partitions [S1].
+Spatial Partitioning for Distributed NeRF is a method to divide 3D space into non-overlapping tiles for distributed training, optimizing for cubic aspect ratios to balance computational load across multiple GPUs [S1]. Unlike Block-NeRF, which partitions based on training camera locations, this method partitions space directly using a Probability Density Function (PDF) derived from scene geometry, allowing for finer bounding boxes in regions with higher content density [S1].
 
